    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The defaults are deliberately conservative. A tool author who forgets to declare concurrency safety gets serial execution. A tool author who forgets to implement permission checks gets the default permission flow. The system fails closed.</p>

  <p>The <code>ToolResult</code> type is worth noting:</p>

  <pre><code>type ToolResult&lt;T&gt; = &#123;
  data: T                    // The actual output
  newMessages?: Message[]    // Optional follow-up messages
  contextModifier?: (ctx) =&gt; ToolUseContext  // Mutate context for next tool
  mcpMeta?: &#123; ... &#125;          // MCP protocol metadata
&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>The <code>contextModifier</code> is important &mdash; it lets a tool change the execution context for subsequent tools in the same turn. This is how tools like <code>EnterWorktree</code> change the working directory for everything that follows. Critically, context modifiers are only allowed for non-concurrency-safe tools. If a tool runs in parallel, it cannot modify shared state.</p>

